            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Takes values measured at scattered points - not on a grid - and fills in a whole grid from them, estimating every cell from the readings around it.\n\nUse it to make a map of a room from sensor readings taken wherever you happened to stand (a magnetic field survey, a light level, a sound level), a heat map of a quantity known only at the performers' positions, or a smooth surface through a few control points to steer a parameter by position.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.griddata <width> <height>\nDefaults: 32 x 32.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nvalues:\nOne value per point. Every input is gridded and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send - so the points can stay put while their readings change.\n\npoints:\nWhere the readings were taken: rows of x y. Held.\n\nwidth, height (int boxes, also inlets):\nHow many cells across and down.\n\nx min, x max, y min, y max (float boxes, also inlets):\nThe area the grid covers, when auto range is off.\n\nmethod:\nAn option, under the node. linear (the default) joins the points with flat triangles;\nnearest gives each cell the value of the closest reading (patches); cubic is smoother within the points; smooth fits a thin-plate spline through every reading - smooth everywhere, and fills the whole grid.\n\nauto range:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), the grid spans the points' own extent.\n\noutside:\nAn option, under the node: what the cells beyond the outermost readings get (linear and cubic cannot reach them) - the nearest reading (the default), nan, or 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ngrid:\nThe filled grid: one row per y, one column per x.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nSix readings at fixed places, with new random values every second. Linear (left) is made of flat triangles with visible creases; smooth (right) flows through the same six readings with no creases.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.interp and scipy.cubic_spline fill in along a line. np.histogram2d counts points into a grid. heat_map draws the grid.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Takes values measured at scattered points - not on a grid - and fills in a whole grid from them, estimating every cell from the readings around it.\n\nUse it to make a map of a room from sensor readings taken wherever you happened to stand (a magnetic field survey, a light level, a sound level), a heat map of a quantity known only at the performers' positions, or a smooth surface through a few control points to steer a parameter by position.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.griddata <width> <height>\nDefaults: 32 x 32.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nvalues:\nOne value per point. Every input is gridded and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send - so the points can stay put while their readings change.\n\npoints:\nWhere the readings were taken: rows of x y. Held.\n\nwidth, height (int boxes, also inlets):\nHow many cells across and down.\n\nx min, x max, y min, y max (float boxes, also inlets):\nThe area the grid covers, when auto range is off.\n\nmethod:\nAn option, under the node. linear (the default) joins the points with flat triangles;\nnearest gives each cell the value of the closest reading (patches); cubic is smoother within the points; smooth fits a thin-plate spline through every reading - smooth everywhere, and fills the whole grid.\n\nauto range:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), the grid spans the points' own extent.\n\noutside:\nAn option, under the node: what the cells beyond the outermost readings get (linear and cubic cannot reach them) - the nearest reading (the default), nan, or 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ngrid:\nThe filled grid: one row per y, one column per x.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nSix readings at fixed places, with new random values every second. Linear (left) is made of flat triangles with visible creases; smooth (right) flows through the same six readings with no creases.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.interp and scipy.cubic_spline fill in along a line. np.histogram2d counts points into a grid. heat_map draws the grid."
        },
